  "why": "The statement is a single universal equality ν=τ for all digraphs; nothing in the record proves it or bounds it after the snapshot. Relaxations (p-adic packing, dyadic or 2^k blow-up) change the question, so they are variants. The chordal Edmonds–Giles theorem with unit weights is exactly Woodall for chordal underlying graphs. I read the authors' remark that the unweighted version 'is still open' as referring to general digraphs, so I scored it a case, digested via JGT. Gutiérrez's dual form transfers to Woodall for planar 3-trees by planar duality, which makes it an unreviewed case. In the repository, PR #210 (three edge-disjoint spanning trees) and PR #215 (k edge-disjoint spanning trees in the condensation components) are genuine class restrictions. They are elementary: a spanning tree meets every cut of the underlying graph. I scored them unreviewed cases generated by Codex. The Codex attribution rests on the branch names and the review-journal quote. The τ=2 PR re-proves folklore, and PR #216 is a composition lemma rather than a settled class, so both are none. Pre-snapshot events: the source-sink, series-parallel and planar-minor cases are digested cases. The CLR ⌊τ/6⌋ result is a pre-snapshot bound, dated by its Nov 2023 preprint.",
